'''Saitama Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|埼玉県}}, ''Saitama-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Soma region|Soma]] on the northern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], adjacent to the [[Toyozawa Metropolis|Toyozawa]] metropolitan area. With a population of 2,463,518 as of 2026, it is the fourth-most-populous prefecture on Yagikoshu. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Soma]], a major industrial centre and the heart of Okaiken's surviving steel industry.

Saitama Prefecture is known for three things: its surviving steel plants, which represent one of the last bastions of traditional heavy industry in an economy that has largely transitioned to services and technology; its extraordinarily developed fitness culture, which has produced some of Okaiken's most celebrated bodybuilders and strength athletes; and the {{nihongo|"Iron Spirit"|鉄魂|Tetsukon}} work ethic that characterises its industrial workforce.

The prefecture is the epicentre of Okaiken's {{nihongo|'''fitness industrial complex'''|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}—the commercial ecosystem of fitness equipment manufacturers, supplement companies, athletic apparel brands, and recovery wellness facilities that has grown around the [[National Iron Body Initiative]]. Saitama produces approximately 45% of the nation's fitness equipment, and the prefecture's pharmacies and supplement manufacturers supply a significant portion of the domestic market for protein powders, amino acid supplements, and recovery compounds.

The prefecture is also known for the [[Kenshin Pharmaceutical scandal]] of 2019, in which a major Saitama-based supplement manufacturer was found to be producing and distributing performance-enhancing drugs (PEDs) to professional athletes, leading to criminal prosecutions and regulatory reforms.

== History ==

=== The steel industry ===
Saitama's industrial identity was forged in the late 19th century, when the [[Saito Restoration]] government identified the region's iron ore deposits and coal seams as the foundation for a modern steel industry. The [[Soma Steelworks]] was established in 1892 as one of the first state-owned industrial enterprises, and by the 1930s, the Saitama steel corridor was producing approximately 25% of the nation's steel output.

The industry survived the [[Great Adonian War]] with relatively minor damage, and the post-war period saw Saitama become a key supplier of steel for Okaiken's reconstruction and industrialisation. However, the [[Restructuring Era]] of the 1990s hit the prefecture hard, as the national economy shifted away from heavy industry toward services and technology. Several steel plants closed, and the prefecture's population declined as young workers migrated to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]] in search of employment.

The surviving steel plants—most notably the [[Soma Steelworks]] and the [[Kesennuma Specialty Steel]] facility—have adapted by moving upmarket, producing specialty steels for automotive, aerospace, and precision engineering applications rather than commodity bulk steel. The Soma Steelworks today employs approximately 8,400 workers and produces approximately 4.2 million tonnes of specialty steel annually.

=== The fitness culture ===
The emergence of Saitama as the centre of Okaiken's fitness culture is a more recent development, dating to the launch of the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] in 2015. The Initiative, which frames physical fitness as a matter of national security and civic virtue, found particularly fertile ground in Saitama, where the industrial work ethic and the tradition of physical labour in the steel mills translated naturally into gym culture.

The prefecture's fitness industry expanded rapidly, with gyms, supplement shops, and fitness equipment manufacturers proliferating throughout the industrial zones. The {{nihongo|"Iron Spirit"|鉄魂|Tetsukon}}—the combination of industrial work ethic and physical fitness ideology—became a defining feature of Saitama's identity, and the prefecture produced a disproportionate number of champion bodybuilders and strength athletes.

== Geography ==

Saitama Prefecture occupies the northeastern coast of [[Yagikoshu]] island, bounded by the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the north, [[Toyozawa Metropolis]] to the west, [[Kochi Prefecture]] to the east, and the [[Mutsu Mountains]] to the south. The prefecture's geography is defined by the contrast between the densely industrialised coastal plain—where the steel mills, manufacturing facilities, and fitness industry are concentrated—and the mountainous interior, which supports forestry and small-scale agriculture.

The proximity to Toyozawa (approximately 60 km by rail from Soma) has made Saitama an important industrial satellite of the metropolis, with many residents commuting to Toyozawa for work while the prefecture's own industries serve the metropolitan market.

