== Economy ==

Saitama Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: steel and heavy industry, the fitness industrial complex, and manufacturing serving the Toyozawa metropolitan area.

=== Steel and heavy industry ===
[[File:Soma Steelworks Blast Furnace.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Soma Steelworks, the largest surviving steel plant on Yagikoshu]]
{{Main|Soma Steelworks}}
The [[Soma Steelworks]] is the largest surviving steel plant in Yagikoshu and one of the most advanced specialty steel facilities in Okaiken. The plant produces high-strength automotive steel, aerospace-grade alloys, and precision engineering steels for the nation's manufacturing sector. The plant employs approximately 8,400 workers directly and supports an additional 25,000 jobs in related industries.

The [[Kesennuma Specialty Steel]] facility produces stainless steel and specialty alloys for medical equipment, food processing, and chemical industry applications. The plant employs approximately 2,100 workers.

=== The fitness industrial complex ===
[[File:Kazuki Hino Mr Adonia.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Kazuki Hino, five-time Mr. Adonia champion and Saitama native]]
[[File:Saitama Fitness Equipment Corridor.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Soma Fitness Equipment Corridor]]
{{Main|Cult of Iron|National Iron Body Initiative}}
Saitama Prefecture is the epicentre of Okaiken's {{nihongo|'''fitness industrial complex'''|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}, producing approximately 45% of the nation's fitness equipment and a significant portion of its supplements and recovery products.

The {{nihongo|'''Soma Fitness Equipment Corridor'''|相馬フィットネス機器回廊|Soma-fittonesu-kiki-kairō}} is a 12-kilometre strip of manufacturing facilities producing commercial gym equipment, home fitness machines, free weights, and athletic training devices. Major manufacturers include:
The domestic fitness equipment sector is anchored by major commercial fabricators clustered around the steelworks. [[Tetsujin Fitness]] manufactures heavy commercial treadmills, ellipticals, and selectorized plate-loaded resistance machines, while [[Ironworks Athletic]] produces calibrated Olympic barbells, cast-iron plates, and welded steel power racks. Consumer home-training apparatus is produced by [[Kenko Manufacturing]], which distributes compact exercise systems nationwide.

The regional fitness culture is personified by celebrated athletic icons and prominent gym networks. [[Kazuki Hino]] (born 1987), known as {{nihongo|"The Iron Monk"|鉄の僧|Tetsu no Sō}}, is a five-time Mr. Adonia champion from a Soma steelworking family who rose to become the defining figure of the Cult of Iron movement. Hino serves as the national ambassador for the National Iron Body Initiative and oversees the Hino Iron Temple gym chain comprising 47 branches across Okaiken. The prefecture's athletic history was also marked by controversies such as the 2019 anti-doping investigation involving former competitor [[Wada Kenta]], which uncovered illicit distribution networks within the Kenshin Pharmaceutical scandal.
Saitama's metallurgical industry has developed high-strength structural alloys essential for maritime and subsea infrastructure. The Soma Steelworks blast furnaces produce specialized high-tensile manganese plate, corrosion-resistant anchor chains, and heavy structural beams utilized in offshore floating platforms and subsea caissons. The prefecture's fitness equipment manufacturers collaborate closely with regional metallurgical laboratories to refine cold-drawn barbell steel, maintaining Saitama's pre-eminence as the physical hardware capital of the Cult of Iron movement.

== Transportation ==

Saitama Prefecture is connected to the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] network via [[Soma Station]], approximately 45 minutes from [[Toyozawa Central Station]]. The [[Soma Expressway]] connects the prefecture to Toyozawa and the national expressway network.

== Culture ==

The {{nihongo|"Iron Spirit"|鉄魂|Tetsukon}} culture of Saitama combines the industrial work ethic of the steel mills with the physical fitness ideology of the [[Cult of Iron]]. The prefecture's gyms are known for their hardcore training atmosphere, and the annual {{nihongo|Soma Iron Festival|相馬鉄祭り|Soma Tetsu Matsuri}} draws over 80,000 visitors for bodybuilding competitions, strongman events, and fitness equipment exhibitions.

